          %h2#references References
          %p The material discussed here corresponds to the most part to sections 3.5 and 3.10 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+a+cohesive+topos"} differential cohomology in a cohesive topos
          %p Exposition:
          %ul
            %li#Loregian19
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fosco+Loregian"}> Fosco Loregian
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Cohesion in Rome
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://tetrapharmakon.github.io/stuff/cohesive_rome.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/LoregianCohesion.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            For references on the general notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos
          %p The following literature is related to or subsumes by the discussion here.
          %p
            Something analogous to the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-connected+%28infinity%2C1%29-site"} ∞-connected site
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+%E2%88%9E-groupoid+in+a+locally+%E2%88%9E-connected+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} fundamental ∞-groupoid in a locally ∞-connected (∞,1)-topos
            is the content of section 2.16. of
          %ul
            %li#SimpsonTeleman
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlos+Simpson"}> Carlos Simpson
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Constantin+Teleman"} Constantin Teleman
              %em
                deRham theorem for
                = succeed "-stacks" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              (1997) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://math.berkeley.edu/~teleman/math/simpson.pdf"}> pdf
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/SimpsonTeleman-deRham.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The
            %a{:href => "#LieTheory"} infinitesimal path ∞-groupoid adjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℜ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi ℑ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi &amp;
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Re \dashv \Im \dashv \&amp;)
            is essentially discussed in section 3 there.
          %p
            The characterization of infinitesimal extensions and formal smoothness by adjoint functors (in 1-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"}> category theory
            ) is considered in
          %ul
            %li#KontsevichRosenbergSpaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"}> Maxim Kontsevich
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Rosenberg"} Alexander Rosenberg
              = succeed "," do
                %em Noncommutative spaces
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://nlab.mathforge.org/nlab/files/KontsevichRosenbergNCSpaces.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.mpim-bonn.mpg.de/preblob/2331"} ps
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.mpim-bonn.mpg.de/preblob/2303"} dvi
          %p
            in the context of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Q-categories"} Q-categories
            \.
          %p
            The notion of forming
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/petit+topos"} petit
            = succeed "-toposes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %ul
            %li#Lurie
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Structured+Spaces"} Structured Spaces
            %li#Carchedi
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Carchedi"}> David Carchedi
                ,
                %em The Meaning of Étale Stacks
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1212.2282"}> arXiv:1212.2282
                )
          %p
            Discussion for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+differential+geometry"} derived differential geometry
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luigi+Alfonsi"}> Luigi Alfonsi
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+A.+S.+Young"} Charles A. S. Young
              %em Towards non-perturbative BV-theory via derived differential cohesive geometry
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.15106"}> arXiv:2307.15106
              \&amp;rbrack;
